Sometimes a function may be given numerically by a table. 
One such example is the notion of windchill - the apparent temperature one feels when taking into account both the acutal air temperature and the speed of the wind. 
A standard table of windchiil values is shown in Figure 2.5. 
From it we swee that if the air temperature is 20 F and thw windspeed is 25 mph, the windchill temperature ("how cold it feels") is 3 F. 
Similarly, if the air temperature is 35 F and the windspeed is 10 mph, then the windchill is 27 F. 
In other words, if s denotes windspeed and t air temperature, then the windchill is a function W(s, t). 

